The '''sociology of knowledge''' studies how social institutions, relationships, historical circumstances and practices shape what people accept, produce and transmit as knowledge.

== Intellectual traditions ==
Karl Mannheim examined relations between social position and thought in ''Ideology and Utopia'' (1929 German edition). Berger and Luckmann's ''The Social Construction of Reality'' (1966) analyzes habitual practices, institutionalization and the transmission of taken-for-granted social meanings. Sociology of scientific knowledge extends investigation to scientific communities, including the conditions under which evidence and consensus are produced. These traditions differ in how they treat objectivity and explanation.

== Methods ==
Ethnography, interviews, historical archives, document analysis and bibliometric networks can investigate how communities stabilize classifications and claims. For example, trace the adoption of a scientific category across laboratories, journals and standards bodies; compare cases with different instrumentation or organizational arrangements. Distinguish observed citation from actual intellectual dependence and allow for competing explanations such as measurement advances.

== Epistemic boundary ==
A causal explanation for belief does not logically establish its truth or falsity. Social epistemology also asks normative questions about testimony, expertise and justified collective inquiry. Institutional trust may be rational yet fallible; consensus may track strong evidence or coordinated error. A sound analysis separates descriptive social mechanisms, evidence for the underlying claim and the normative evaluation of knowledge practices.

